 "description": "Change the caller's private address book of people \u2014 crew and contacts (create, update, delete, unlink_account, mark_self, unmark_self, invite, accept_invitation). Personal data (birth date, passport) is never shared across users. `linkedAccount` reports whether this person has accepted an invitation and is linked to a Seamark account. `isSelf` marks the ONE person in the caller's book that is the caller, so voyages with them on the crew list count as the caller's own. `invite` mails a Seamark invitation to a person who crews at least one of the caller's trips (409 `invitation_no_trips` otherwise \u2014 the person must be on a trip's crew first); accepting it links their account to this person, which opens EVERY trip of the caller's they crew, past and future, so the mail lists all of them. It is rate-limited to one send per person per minute and 3 sends per recipient address per hour (a budget shared with trip_write's `announce`, keyed on the address rather than the feature), remembers the address on the person's `email` (advancing the person's `updatedAt` version token \u2014 the response carries the new value), and re-inviting replaces the previous invitation, so a mistyped address's link stops working. `accept_invitation` is the other side: it binds the CALLER's own account to the person named by the token, requires the caller's address to match the one invited, and returns every trip it opened. `unlink_account` revokes a person's account link everywhere at once AND cancels any invitation to that person that has not been accepted yet \u2014 a pending invite mail stops working, which is what makes the revocation final rather than something the other party can undo by re-clicking their link. It is idempotent, and on someone who was never linked its only effect is to cancel a pending invitation. `mark_self` sets `isSelf` (marking someone new moves the marker off whoever held it \u2014 at most one person is the caller), `unmark_self` clears it. Both are idempotent and unrelated to `linkedAccount`/invitations \u2014 \"this is me\" is the caller's own identity, not an access grant. IDs are 6-character case-sensitive alphanumeric strings. Timestamps are UTC ISO 8601. Distances are integer meters. Durations are integer seconds.",
